.===============================================================================
.TITLE{Layout #3 WK - Package-Oriented Design}

William Kennedy from Ardian Labs "Package-Oriented Design" (2017)

.notes
Very popular in enterprise Go.
The internal/ directory is enforced by the Go compiler.
.notes.end

.===============================================================================
.TITLE{Kennedy - Directory structure}

```shell
.
├── cmd/
│   └── library/
│       └── main.go
├── internal/
│   ├── platform/
│   │   ├── database/
│   │   │   └── postgres.go
│   │   └── http/
│   │       └── server.go
│   ├── users/
│   │   ├── model.go
│   │   └── service.go
│   ├── books/
│   │   ├── model.go
│   │   └── service.go
│   └── reviews/
│       ├── model.go
│       └── service.go
└── go.mod
```

.===============================================================================
.TITLE{Kennedy - Three layers of packages}

**1. Domain packages** (`internal/users/`, `internal/books/`, `internal/reviews/`)
- Business logic, models, service interfaces
- The core of the application

.transition

**2. Platform packages** (`internal/platform/`)
- Foundational, reusable infrastructure - database, HTTP server, middleware
- NOT business-specific

.transition

**3. Composition root** (`cmd/library/`)
- Wires domain and platform together

.===============================================================================
.TITLE{Kennedy - Domain package}

```go
// internal/users/model.go
package users

type User struct {
    ID    int
    Name  string
    Email string
}
```

```go
// internal/users/service.go
package users

import "database/sql"

type Service struct {
    DB *sql.DB
}

func (s *Service) User(id int) (User, error) {
    return User{}, nil
}
```

.===============================================================================
.TITLE{Kennedy - Platform & composition root}

```go
// internal/platform/database/postgres.go
package database

import "database/sql"

func Open(connString string) (*sql.DB, error) {
    return sql.Open("postgres", connString)
}
```

```go
// cmd/library/main.go
package main

import (
    "log"
    "os"
    "github.com/example/internal/platform/database"
    "github.com/example/internal/users"
)

func main() {
    db, _ := database.Open(os.Getenv("DB"))
    userService := users.Service{DB: db}
    // ... wire handlers, start server
}
```

.===============================================================================
.TITLE{Kennedy - When it works}

- Medium-to-large applications
- In multi team with multi repo setup `internal/` enforces boundaries
- Enterprise/Corporate Go

.===============================================================================
.TITLE{Kennedy - When it breaks}

- `internal/platform/` can become a "junk drawer"
- Domain packages **may** eq. directly import `database/sql`, nothing is preventing "infrastructure" to leak into domain
- No prescribed way to **share** common libraries between repos
- Overkill for small services
